-- title:
--   BoringSSL poly1305.cc compiled for AArch64, as a placed library
-- statement:
--   The object code of BoringSSL's generic Poly1305, `crypto/poly1305/poly1305.cc` at commit `f8b3e234`, as a placed library in the `Arm64` semantics (google-deepmind/code-verification's `Arm64.Trusted`, republished as the `Def_Arm64_*` bundles). This 26-bit-limb code (by Andrew Moon, “poly1305-donna”) is the Poly1305 that BoringSSL builds for 64-bit ARM: its NEON variant is for 32-bit ARM only.
--
--   **The build.** `poly1305.cc` was compiled with zig cc 0.16.0 for `aarch64-linux-musl` at `-O2`, using the flags of DeepMind's `rust-obj2lean print-cflags`, plus `-fno-builtin-memset`. Without that flag the compiler turns the zero-padding loop of the internal `poly1305_update` (lines 131–133) into a call to the C library's `memset`, an external function. The object file's SHA-256 is `74bbe85a…3bec`, and the build is reproducible (`Implementation/build.sh` in the mission repository). `rust-obj2lean export` turned it into this module. Its byte sidecar is inlined as a literal list, its imports are renamed to the published bundles, and its docstring's account of the upstream build is replaced (the file says so).
--
--   **The library `boringsslPoly1305Lib`.**
--   - `boringsslPoly1305Text` is the code block: the 1,136 bytes (284 instructions) of `boringsslPoly1305PlacedCode`, four function symbols with their offsets (`CRYPTO_poly1305_init` at `0x0`, `CRYPTO_poly1305_update` at `0x78`, the internal `poly1305_update` at `0x16c` under its mangled name, `CRYPTO_poly1305_finish` at `0x340`), and the three relocations the linker resolves: the calls of the internal `poly1305_update` from `CRYPTO_poly1305_update` (two) and `CRYPTO_poly1305_finish` (one).
--   - There are no data objects (`boringsslPoly1305Data` is empty) and no external symbols.
--   - The three functions `CRYPTO_poly1305_init`, `CRYPTO_poly1305_update` and `CRYPTO_poly1305_finish` are the exports.
--
--   The statements quantify over every placement of this library that the `Arm64` model accepts (`Obj.PlacedLib.Placeable`), so no load address is fixed.
